Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x^2$, let's divide by $x^2$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $\sqrt{x^4}$, since for any value, $x^2=\sqrt{x^4}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{5x^2+6x}{\sqrt{16x^4-5x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5x^2+6x}{x^2}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{16x^4-5x^2}}{\sqrt{x^4}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x^2=\sqrt{x^4}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{6\cancel x}{\cancel x \cdot x}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{16\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}-\dfrac{5\cancel {x^2}}{\cancel {x^2}\cdot x^2}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{5+\dfrac{6}{x}}{\sqrt{16-\dfrac{5}{x^2}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{5+0}{\sqrt{16-0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5}{\sqrt{16}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5}{4}
\end{align}$
